1
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:11,190
JUJUTSU KAISEN

2
00:00:34,470 --> 00:00:38,680
JUJUTSU KAISEN
The Culling Game Part 1

3
00:01:29,990 --> 00:01:32,740
In Japan, 99.9% of Criminal Trials End in a Guilty Verdict

4
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:44,270
I owned a cat.

5
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:46,830
Continue.

6
00:01:47,890 --> 00:01:50,600
A cop suddenly started speaking to me.

7
00:01:51,050 --> 00:01:53,250
Then there were two, then three of them.

8
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:55,330
I thought, "If they take me in now,

9
00:01:55,330 --> 00:01:56,830
my cat is going to die."

10
00:01:57,350 --> 00:01:59,150
My place doesn't allow us to own them.

11
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:02,470
So I was the only one who knew about it.

12
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:05,270
In Morioka City, Iwate Prefecture,

13
00:02:05,270 --> 00:02:07,230
in March of 2016,

14
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:09,610
two people—
a young girl and her mother—

15
00:02:09,610 --> 00:02:13,050
were found fatally stabbed
in their own home.

16
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:15,990
The Morioka Prosecutor's Office

17
00:02:15,990 --> 00:02:18,130
indicted Keita Oe, who lived nearby,

18
00:02:18,130 --> 00:02:21,290
on charges of felony murder and robbery.

19
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:23,810
The events leading to
his arrest were thus:

20
00:02:24,330 --> 00:02:28,540
Oe fled during police questioning
by officers on patrol,

21
00:02:28,090 --> 00:02:29,250
Hey! Stop!

22
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:30,460
and after police chased
him into his home,

23
00:02:30,930 --> 00:02:35,010
the officers who chased him discovered
a blade with blood on it,

24
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:37,970
and then arrested him
in flagrante delicto.

25
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:41,840
Later DNA analysis

26
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:41,040
There he is!

27
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:41,840
Come on, behave!

28
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:45,310
revealed the blood on the blade
matched the victims' DNA.

29
00:02:44,190 --> 00:02:45,310
Stop struggling!

30
00:02:46,690 --> 00:02:49,630
Oe consistently denied all
culpability for the crime.

31
00:02:49,630 --> 00:02:52,080
On the blade collected
as evidence, he insisted...

32
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:53,420
I just picked it up.

33
00:02:53,770 --> 00:02:54,780
It wasn't mine.

34
00:02:55,590 --> 00:02:57,910
That's a bit hard to believe, isn't it?

35
00:02:57,910 --> 00:03:01,830
I mean, he refused the questioning
because he felt guilty, right?

36
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:05,510
No. He was previously a victim
of wrongful questioning

37
00:03:05,510 --> 00:03:09,380
because an acquaintance of his
was using drugs in the past.

38
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:11,890
You're saying that traumatized him?

39
00:03:11,890 --> 00:03:14,260
Even so, "I picked it up"?

40
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:17,440
A bloody kitchen knife? Why?

41
00:03:17,890 --> 00:03:21,100
He claims he was planning to
turn it in to the police later.

42
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:22,320
"Later"?!

43
00:03:23,330 --> 00:03:24,710
I know.

44
00:03:24,710 --> 00:03:26,310
His claims are all hard to believe.

45
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:29,320
But when you consider Oe's background—

46
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:33,300
the fact that he works as a live-in
employee at a non-profit organization—

47
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:35,530
it starts sounding plausible.

48
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:37,780
A non-profit?

49
00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:40,900
They work with the elderly
who have no place to go,

50
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:44,430
primarily running a shelter and providing
support for their independent living.

51
00:03:44,430 --> 00:03:47,910
Oe's job was taking care
of the elderly there.

52
00:03:48,330 --> 00:03:51,160
However, they didn't pay him a wage.

53
00:03:51,630 --> 00:03:54,590
They only supplied him with goods
like groceries, boxed lunches,

54
00:03:54,590 --> 00:03:57,350
and the occasional New Year's
gift of pocket money.

55
00:03:57,680 --> 00:03:59,410
Yet despite that,

56
00:03:59,410 --> 00:04:02,850
they collected 50,000 yen
a month from him as rent.

57
00:04:02,850 --> 00:04:03,950
Brutal.

58
00:04:04,370 --> 00:04:07,940
Apparently, quite a few of the
residents have prior records.

59
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:11,640
Furthermore, the reason he was delayed in
turning over the weapon he found was...

60
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:15,650
The rules say we can't call
any cop cars or ambulances.

61
00:04:15,950 --> 00:04:18,010
Because they'd bother the neighbors.

62
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:34,420
Higuruma Law Office

63
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:23,060
That organization sounds
kinda shady, doesn't it?

64
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:23,960
Yeah.

65
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:26,370
It's extremely close to
being outright illegal.

66
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:30,740
We've already seen an increase
in suspiciously managed NPOs

67
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:33,920
since the earthquake relief funds
started flowing into this region.

68
00:04:34,420 --> 00:04:37,270
So it's not surprising the murder
weapon was just lying around.

69
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:41,420
There's a good possibility Oe's innocent.

70
00:04:43,060 --> 00:04:46,470
First, we'll investigate the
organization's activities and tax filings,

71
00:04:46,470 --> 00:04:49,350
then run background checks
on all the residents.

72
00:04:49,350 --> 00:04:52,280
I'll focus on getting
information out of Oe.

73
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:54,070
You handle the investigation, Shimizu.

74
00:04:54,070 --> 00:04:57,570
You're dumping a huge job like
that on me out of the blue?

75
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:01,080
Could you take care of his cat, too?

76
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:02,160
The cat?!

77
00:05:03,110 --> 00:05:06,780
The commission for public defense cases
is low, despite how much work they take.

78
00:05:07,110 --> 00:05:09,360
The public assumes he's guilty, too.

79
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:12,120
Plus, there's no suspended
sentence in murder cases,

80
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:13,800
and he could receive the death penalty.

81
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:15,750
There's nothing to gain here.

82
00:05:16,910 --> 00:05:21,970
Jeez, Higuruma-san! Why did you
go and accept a case like this?

83
00:05:21,970 --> 00:05:23,430
It's fine every now and then.

84
00:05:23,890 --> 00:05:26,540
If we stuck to easy work,
our skills would dull.

85
00:05:26,810 --> 00:05:28,700
It's more than every now and then!

86
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:30,860
It's more than every now and then!

87
00:05:31,370 --> 00:05:33,770
You're saying your thoughts out loud.

88
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:38,970
Sounds like Higuruma-kun
hasn't changed then.

89
00:05:39,540 --> 00:05:42,860
It's rough when an independent
lawyer isn't in it for the money.

90
00:05:43,320 --> 00:05:48,620
At least it's not so bad that I'm being
told to run a café on the side like you.

91
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:50,500
But when you say he hasn't changed,

92
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:52,910
you mean Higuruma-san's
always been like this?

93
00:05:52,910 --> 00:05:54,660
Hm? No.

94
00:05:55,130 --> 00:05:57,770
I think he actually used to be worse.

95
00:05:57,770 --> 00:05:58,670
Huh?

96
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:01,390
Back when he was with us,

97
00:06:01,390 --> 00:06:04,510
he was in charge of a case of
reckless driving resulting in injury.

98
00:06:04,510 --> 00:06:06,360
Oh, a drunk driving one.

99
00:06:07,260 --> 00:06:09,620
The defendant was nineteen,

100
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:14,320
and his coworkers had coerced him into
both the drinking and the driving.

101
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:17,220
However, those involved all
aligned their stories,

102
00:06:17,220 --> 00:06:19,150
and he wasn't able to prepare
money for a settlement,

103
00:06:19,150 --> 00:06:21,640
nor were we able to get
him a suspended sentence.

104
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:28,640
Liar!

105
00:06:30,370 --> 00:06:33,120
You said they'd find me innocent!

106
00:06:33,830 --> 00:06:37,840
You told him you'd manage to get
a suspended sentence, though,

107
00:06:37,840 --> 00:06:40,320
not an innocent verdict.

108
00:06:40,990 --> 00:06:42,740
Have you learned your lesson now?

109
00:06:42,740 --> 00:06:45,980
Quit being a criminal defense
lawyer for impossible cases.

110
00:06:46,560 --> 00:06:50,600
Trying to save the weak
may be a noble endeavor,

111
00:06:50,990 --> 00:06:53,550
but there's a limit to what
lawyers like us can do

112
00:06:53,550 --> 00:06:56,080
without understanding from
the courts and prosecutors.

113
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:57,290
Oh, I want to ride that!

114
00:06:56,530 --> 00:07:00,020
It's not worth continuing when
your clients resent you.

115
00:07:01,780 --> 00:07:05,980
They're at the end of their rope,
both mentally and financially.

116
00:07:06,390 --> 00:07:08,690
I can't blame them for lashing out at me.

117
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:14,490
But what about your mental
state, Higuruma-kun?

118
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:16,610
I...

119
00:07:38,750 --> 00:07:41,500
We find the defendant...

120
00:07:41,940 --> 00:07:43,200
innocent.

121
00:07:48,610 --> 00:07:50,520
We actually won this case?

122
00:07:52,410 --> 00:07:53,650
Not yet.

123
00:07:55,170 --> 00:07:57,020
They'll appeal right away.

124
00:07:57,410 --> 00:07:58,900
The Morioka Mother-Child Murder Incident
Questionable Innocent Verdict.
"It Was Bought"
Former Judge Comments on Crooked Lawyer's Statements

125
00:08:00,710 --> 00:08:02,420
The verdict was innocent.
Guess even murderers go free
if they can pay up.
How much did he dump on this...
The law is dead. Prosecutors are useless.

126
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:04,170
Refusing to Accept the Current Ruling,
They Have Appealed Seeking A Revision

127
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:04,170
Morioka Prosecutor's Office

128
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:04,170
The prosecution has...

129
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:17,800
Nothing stolen was found
in Oe-san's room.

130
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:22,770
There was another person in the same NPO
who went missing right after the crime.

131
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:26,570
The fact that you were caught on a
convenience store's security camera feed

132
00:08:26,570 --> 00:08:30,920
during the time of the crime determined
from the corpse played a major role, too.

133
00:08:31,350 --> 00:08:34,410
Good thing you don't cook
for yourself, Oe-san!

134
00:08:34,910 --> 00:08:36,310
Counselor Higuruma...

135
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:38,460
Thank you so much.

136
00:08:38,990 --> 00:08:41,430
No need for "Counselor."

137
00:08:41,430 --> 00:08:44,570
Besides, we're not in the clear yet.
We still have the second trial.

138
00:08:45,010 --> 00:08:46,210
I worked hard, too.

139
00:08:45,790 --> 00:08:47,210
That's not what I mean.

140
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,170
Thank you for believing me!

141
00:08:56,560 --> 00:08:59,060
Appeal Trial (Second Trial)

142
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:02,060
Guilty. Life Sentence.

143
00:09:02,370 --> 00:09:07,200
While defense lawyers have to work
with limited budgets and manpower,

144
00:09:07,200 --> 00:09:11,310
prosecutors can pour manpower and
taxpayer money into digging up evidence.

145
00:09:11,310 --> 00:09:13,680
That's fine. That's how the system works.

146
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:15,070
However,

147
00:09:15,330 --> 00:09:19,270
the prosecutors didn't submit any new
evidence during the second trial.

148
00:09:21,270 --> 00:09:24,960
The judge recognized
their absurd claim that

149
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:27,510
"there's no reason to suspect the
missing person of the crime,"

150
00:09:27,510 --> 00:09:29,460
and they overturned the verdict.

151
00:09:31,370 --> 00:09:34,470
The highest court is always the
bottleneck for a third trial.

152
00:09:34,470 --> 00:09:37,060
Most final appeals are
turned away at the door.

153
00:09:37,060 --> 00:09:39,990
It's challenging to even
get a proper trial there.

154
00:09:40,850 --> 00:09:44,370
This trial was destined for a
guilty verdict from the start.

155
00:09:51,670 --> 00:09:53,030
Why are you

156
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:56,690
looking at me that way?

157
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:03,620
But what about your mental
state, Higuruma-kun?

158
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:08,980
I'm not trying to tout the
ideal of saving the weak.

159
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:12,140
It's just always gone against my nature

160
00:10:12,140 --> 00:10:14,430
to leave things alone when
I feel like they're wrong.

161
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:17,580
I've just never managed
to fix that habit.

162
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:22,750
The goddess of justice blinds her sight
to maintain fairness under the law,

163
00:10:22,750 --> 00:10:26,560
and people turn a blind eye to all
kinds of things to protect themselves.

164
00:10:26,970 --> 00:10:31,260
But I don't want to brush away the
hands clinging to me for help,

165
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:37,730
so I want to keep my eyes open, at least.

166
00:10:42,060 --> 00:10:44,760
If there are any objections
to today's trial,

167
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:49,630
submit your final appeal in writing
within fifteen days, including today.

168
00:11:01,180 --> 00:11:02,890
Higuruma-san?

169
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:08,310
Everyone come back.

170
00:11:13,260 --> 00:11:15,090
We're having a retrial.

171
00:11:15,570 --> 00:11:16,520
Culling Game Player

172
00:11:16,520 --> 00:11:19,280
Hiromi Higuruma

173
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:20,540
Higuruma is in...

174
00:11:20,540 --> 00:11:21,620
...Shinjuku.

175
00:11:21,620 --> 00:11:22,410
...Ikebukuro.

176
00:11:22,410 --> 00:11:23,030
Got it?

177
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:24,490
Shinjuku?

178
00:11:24,890 --> 00:11:27,090
I hope Itadori is heading there, too.

179
00:11:27,090 --> 00:11:28,610
Ikebukuro, huh?

180
00:11:28,610 --> 00:11:30,330
I wonder if Fushiguro's there, too.

181
00:11:34,370 --> 00:11:36,910
Isn't she a little late?

182
00:11:36,910 --> 00:11:39,340
When is that girl

183
00:11:39,340 --> 00:11:43,260
going to bring me my next sucker?

184
00:11:44,010 --> 00:11:47,010
Episode 55
Tokyo No. 1 Colony, Part 2

185
00:11:50,550 --> 00:11:53,150
Huh? Wh-What?

186
00:11:53,150 --> 00:11:55,240
Oh, no, no.

187
00:11:55,240 --> 00:11:56,860
Not like that at all.

188
00:11:56,860 --> 00:11:59,470
Hey, what are you doing?

189
00:11:59,870 --> 00:12:01,370
Like this! This!

190
00:12:01,370 --> 00:12:03,120
R-Right.

191
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:04,860
Why are you keeping quiet?

192
00:12:05,130 --> 00:12:07,620
You're better off laughing!

193
00:12:08,610 --> 00:12:10,460
Come on, come on!

194
00:12:11,060 --> 00:12:13,430
Oh, dear. What did I tell you?

195
00:12:12,150 --> 00:12:13,350
What's your problem?

196
00:12:13,350 --> 00:12:13,990
What's your problem?!

197
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,710
Not that I actually told you.

198
00:12:14,470 --> 00:12:15,630
There you go.

199
00:12:16,250 --> 00:12:18,070
But why can't you understand?

200
00:12:16,890 --> 00:12:18,280
What's your problem?!

201
00:12:18,070 --> 00:12:19,550
Are you an idiot?!

202
00:12:18,540 --> 00:12:19,980
What's your problem?!

203
00:12:19,550 --> 00:12:22,750
These guys aren't that smart or athletic,

204
00:12:22,750 --> 00:12:25,340
but they're brimming with pride.

205
00:12:25,340 --> 00:12:29,850
You just have to match their vibe and
joke around so you don't hurt that pride.

206
00:12:30,490 --> 00:12:34,100
Then you won't get targeted
by anyone else, either.

207
00:12:34,100 --> 00:12:36,550
And you can occasionally
enjoy a good time, too.

208
00:12:37,480 --> 00:12:38,760
Stop it.

209
00:12:42,020 --> 00:12:43,980
Here comes another idiot.

210
00:12:43,980 --> 00:12:46,320
Is that uniform from West Middle?

211
00:12:46,320 --> 00:12:48,070
You're a middle schooler?

212
00:12:48,070 --> 00:12:50,580
You weren't showing respect, were you?

213
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:53,450
So you need to

214
00:12:53,450 --> 00:12:54,660
learn a lesson!

215
00:12:55,400 --> 00:12:56,250
Huh?

216
00:13:01,700 --> 00:13:02,700
Huh?

217
00:13:10,290 --> 00:13:12,550
No violence!

218
00:13:16,970 --> 00:13:18,520
Come here!

219
00:13:29,250 --> 00:13:30,300
What about you?

220
00:13:30,610 --> 00:13:31,610
Culling Game Player

221
00:13:33,740 --> 00:13:35,740
M-Me?

222
00:13:35,740 --> 00:13:37,750
Yuji Itadori
Rin Amai

223
00:13:36,810 --> 00:13:37,750
Hey.

224
00:13:37,750 --> 00:13:39,100
What?

225
00:13:39,100 --> 00:13:42,320
You aren't planning to go through
Ikebukuro to reach Shinjuku, are you?

226
00:13:42,880 --> 00:13:44,740
That area's rich with supplies.

227
00:13:45,230 --> 00:13:47,640
We're more likely to
encounter other Players.

228
00:13:47,900 --> 00:13:50,630
There are two reasons for going this way.

229
00:13:50,630 --> 00:13:52,550
The route is easy to follow,

230
00:13:52,550 --> 00:13:54,790
and I have a base nearby.

231
00:13:56,340 --> 00:13:58,690
At least let me take a shower.

232
00:13:58,690 --> 00:14:01,340
I haven't gotten much
rest since yesterday.

233
00:14:04,340 --> 00:14:07,400
Higuruma uses this theater as his base.

234
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:09,450
Assuming he hasn't moved, that is.

235
00:14:09,450 --> 00:14:10,710
Cool!

236
00:14:10,710 --> 00:14:13,020
You've been a big help! Thanks!

237
00:14:13,550 --> 00:14:16,130
Oh, h-h-hold on!

238
00:14:16,450 --> 00:14:17,490
What?

239
00:14:17,850 --> 00:14:19,650
Are you seriously going?

240
00:14:19,650 --> 00:14:23,460
I'd rather reunite with my friend
first, but we're in a hurry.

241
00:14:23,790 --> 00:14:26,510
I haven't met Higuruma myself,

242
00:14:26,510 --> 00:14:29,970
but he once beat Haba-san to a pulp.

243
00:14:29,970 --> 00:14:31,590
Then I'll be fine!

244
00:14:31,590 --> 00:14:35,270
I beat that propeller head, too,
so I'll at least be able to run away.

245
00:14:35,970 --> 00:14:37,220
Maybe so

246
00:14:36,840 --> 00:14:37,960
See you later!

247
00:14:39,730 --> 00:14:40,950
Say it!

248
00:14:42,220 --> 00:14:43,760
Say it, man!

249
00:14:44,680 --> 00:14:46,760
You should stop doing this!

250
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:53,690
I'm sorry,

251
00:14:54,880 --> 00:14:56,120
Itadori.

252
00:14:58,970 --> 00:15:02,460
Higuruma? Oh...

253
00:15:02,460 --> 00:15:06,130
Sorry, but I'm Reggie.

254
00:15:06,130 --> 00:15:09,190
She deceived you.

255
00:15:12,710 --> 00:15:14,480
Don't give me that look.

256
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:16,780
I'm not frightened at all.

257
00:15:16,780 --> 00:15:18,430
Just so you know, Reggie—

258
00:15:18,430 --> 00:15:19,230
Stop talking.

259
00:15:23,180 --> 00:15:24,370
Oho?

260
00:15:25,960 --> 00:15:27,820
We're wasting time.

261
00:15:38,710 --> 00:15:39,740
Who's there?

262
00:15:40,190 --> 00:15:42,240
What are you doing over there?

263
00:15:44,130 --> 00:15:45,360
I could ask you that.

264
00:15:48,120 --> 00:15:51,900
Have you ever taken a bath
while in your clothes?

265
00:15:51,900 --> 00:15:53,010
Never.

266
00:15:53,820 --> 00:15:56,940
It feels better than I expected.

267
00:15:59,520 --> 00:16:00,630
Right.

268
00:16:02,550 --> 00:16:04,540
Back when I was in elementary school,

269
00:16:04,540 --> 00:16:07,580
I loved our classes on
swimming in clothes.

270
00:16:08,230 --> 00:16:11,830
So many things have stopped
mattering lately,

271
00:16:11,830 --> 00:16:16,440
so I've been challenging myself to
try things I assumed I shouldn't do.

272
00:16:16,440 --> 00:16:19,990
I've basically gone off the
rails after hitting my mid-30s.

273
00:16:19,990 --> 00:16:21,040
Is that funny?

274
00:16:21,590 --> 00:16:23,530
It's kind of amusing.

275
00:16:24,050 --> 00:16:25,870
You're Higuruma, right?

276
00:16:26,350 --> 00:16:27,820
In the flesh.

277
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:28,960
I want to talk.

278
00:16:29,780 --> 00:16:31,930
Hold on. Wait, wait.

279
00:16:32,260 --> 00:16:34,060
I'm a lawyer.

280
00:16:34,060 --> 00:16:38,670
Talking to me will incur a consultation
fee of 5,000 yen every half hour.

281
00:16:38,670 --> 00:16:39,520
Huh?!

282
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:42,810
I'm kidding.

283
00:16:42,810 --> 00:16:45,820
I just wanted to try acting
like a nasty lawyer for once.

284
00:16:46,860 --> 00:16:48,350
I see.

285
00:16:48,890 --> 00:16:50,930
What's this guy doing?

286
00:16:51,550 --> 00:16:53,520
You have 100 points, right?

287
00:16:54,190 --> 00:16:56,620
He's not a past sorcerer
who's been incarnated.

288
00:16:56,870 --> 00:16:59,900
He's a modern sorcerer whose
technique was activated!

289
00:16:59,900 --> 00:17:01,410
We can talk it out!

290
00:17:01,410 --> 00:17:02,940
There's room for negotiation!

291
00:17:03,360 --> 00:17:05,610
Oh, yeah. You pass.

292
00:17:05,960 --> 00:17:06,750
Huh?

293
00:17:06,750 --> 00:17:09,010
That's enough. I can tell now.

294
00:17:09,010 --> 00:17:10,960
That's quite the shikigami.

295
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:12,520
You're strong, right?

296
00:17:12,520 --> 00:17:16,310
If you were weak, I'd just
kill you as another mark.

297
00:17:16,310 --> 00:17:17,520
But you're good.

298
00:17:17,520 --> 00:17:18,890
Let's work together.

299
00:17:18,890 --> 00:17:20,240
What gives?!

300
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:21,830
I never heard about this!

301
00:17:22,960 --> 00:17:24,810
What are you getting at?

302
00:17:24,810 --> 00:17:28,470
How much do you know
about this Culling Game?

303
00:17:29,170 --> 00:17:30,450
It's a ritual, right?

304
00:17:30,970 --> 00:17:34,220
It uses the cursed energy of
the Players inside the barriers

305
00:17:34,220 --> 00:17:36,790
to ferry the people of
Japan to the other side

306
00:17:36,790 --> 00:17:39,080
and change them into something inhuman.

307
00:17:39,710 --> 00:17:42,170
Guess I should stay quiet
about Tengen-sama.

308
00:17:42,170 --> 00:17:45,810
Oh? Do you know Kenjaku, then?

309
00:17:46,870 --> 00:17:48,670
I knew there'd be one!

310
00:17:49,130 --> 00:17:51,650
A sorcerer connected to Kenjaku!

311
00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:54,780
Uh, so, to be frank,

312
00:17:55,590 --> 00:17:59,000
we want to stop the Culling Game.

313
00:17:59,770 --> 00:18:01,050
Er, time out.

314
00:18:01,050 --> 00:18:04,550
When I say "stop it," I mean we want
to negate the compulsory killing.

315
00:18:04,950 --> 00:18:08,790
Please let us use your 100 points
to add a rule toward that end.

316
00:18:09,310 --> 00:18:11,440
Allow me to be frank, as well.

317
00:18:12,010 --> 00:18:13,080
I refuse.

318
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:16,290
Is that another joke?

319
00:18:17,100 --> 00:18:18,170
No.

320
00:18:18,170 --> 00:18:21,700
I just sense potential
in the Culling Game.

321
00:18:21,700 --> 00:18:24,160
Complete Book of the Six Codes

322
00:18:22,130 --> 00:18:24,160
Sometimes the law is powerless.

323
00:18:24,160 --> 00:18:27,620
But what about the rules
of this Culling Game?

324
00:18:27,620 --> 00:18:30,210
If the power I've been granted is real,

325
00:18:30,210 --> 00:18:32,090
then those rules must be, too.

326
00:18:32,090 --> 00:18:36,460
What you just said about Players'
cursed energy being used?

327
00:18:36,460 --> 00:18:38,290
That's probably a bluff.

328
00:18:38,290 --> 00:18:39,100
What?

329
00:18:39,100 --> 00:18:44,280
Well, less of a bluff,
and more Plan B or C.

330
00:18:44,280 --> 00:18:46,060
I have three reasons.

331
00:18:46,560 --> 00:18:48,250
The number of Players.

332
00:18:48,250 --> 00:18:50,150
The strength gap between Players.

333
00:18:50,150 --> 00:18:51,850
The barriers' rules.

334
00:18:52,290 --> 00:18:57,320
If roughly 1,000 people were equally
split up between the ten Colonies,

335
00:18:57,320 --> 00:18:59,850
that would be 100 per Colony.

336
00:18:59,850 --> 00:19:03,130
But among the Players are sorcerers

337
00:19:03,130 --> 00:19:05,160
like Kashimo or Higuruma,

338
00:19:05,160 --> 00:19:06,810
who are much stronger than the rest.

339
00:19:06,810 --> 00:19:10,770
These two alone have already
culled sixty sorcerers.

340
00:19:10,770 --> 00:19:14,370
There's probably one like
them in each Colony.

341
00:19:14,790 --> 00:19:17,610
So the weak are weeded out early on,

342
00:19:17,610 --> 00:19:19,990
and the Culling Game
will reach a stalemate?

343
00:19:20,670 --> 00:19:22,630
I certainly did sense that.

344
00:19:22,990 --> 00:19:25,290
The Colony has been too quiet.

345
00:19:25,290 --> 00:19:26,890
It already has.

346
00:19:26,890 --> 00:19:29,930
At least here in Tokyo No. 1.

347
00:19:29,930 --> 00:19:33,820
On top of that, there's the teleportation
when people enter the Colony.

348
00:19:33,820 --> 00:19:36,070
It serves to spread them out,

349
00:19:36,070 --> 00:19:38,140
but its main goal is likely to encourage

350
00:19:38,140 --> 00:19:40,610
an early second awakening
of modern sorcerers.

351
00:19:40,610 --> 00:19:43,330
There are some who die during
the teleportation, too.

352
00:19:43,330 --> 00:19:46,240
If he was really trying to use
the Players' cursed energy,

353
00:19:46,240 --> 00:19:48,870
he would want the greatest
number of sorcerers

354
00:19:48,870 --> 00:19:51,580
to fight for the longest time possible.

355
00:19:51,580 --> 00:19:56,330
What if people who broke the
rules were punished by physics,

356
00:19:56,330 --> 00:20:00,330
without any need for indictments,
prosecutions, or arguments at trials?

357
00:20:00,330 --> 00:20:02,650
Wouldn't that be wonderful?

358
00:20:02,650 --> 00:20:04,940
I'll acknowledge there are
issues with the current rules,

359
00:20:04,940 --> 00:20:08,300
but I want to watch over the system
serving as a basis for this game.

360
00:20:08,300 --> 00:20:10,800
It won't do for it to end too soon.

361
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:13,750
I'd especially love to witness
the stripping of a technique

362
00:20:10,810 --> 00:20:16,560
2: Players breaching the first rule
shall be stripped of their technique.
8:  Players who do not show a change in points
within 19 days of joining or acquiring points
shall be stripped of their technique.

363
00:20:13,750 --> 00:20:16,560
mentioned in Rule 2 and 8 at least once.

364
00:20:16,940 --> 00:20:19,870
It's better for the rules if
being stripped means death, too.

365
00:20:20,320 --> 00:20:23,220
The Culling Game itself is a ritual.

366
00:20:23,220 --> 00:20:26,300
If we take too long,
every person in this nation will die.

367
00:20:26,300 --> 00:20:27,650
That's a lie.

368
00:20:27,650 --> 00:20:30,740
The Culling Game insists
on its own permanence.

369
00:20:30,740 --> 00:20:33,110
I don't really get that part, either!

370
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:37,180
Let me rephrase it, then.

371
00:20:37,910 --> 00:20:41,300
Let me use your 100 points, Higuruma!

372
00:20:42,720 --> 00:20:46,670
Have you ever killed someone
who ticked you off?

373
00:20:49,810 --> 00:20:54,160
It feels much better than I expected.

374
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:56,210
A shikigami?!

375
00:20:57,360 --> 00:20:59,210
Higuruma has 100 points.

376
00:20:59,210 --> 00:21:01,640
He might have killed
twenty sorcerers already.

377
00:21:02,190 --> 00:21:03,870
I can't leap in recklessly.

378
00:21:04,330 --> 00:21:05,690
I'll go on defense,

379
00:21:05,690 --> 00:21:08,180
but I'll be ready to
respond to any attack!

380
00:21:10,170 --> 00:21:11,780
Domain Expansion.

381
00:21:12,650 --> 00:21:14,730
Deadly Sentencing.

382
00:21:26,900 --> 00:21:32,430
The rules' idea of continuation is
just a cover for the real plan.

383
00:21:32,430 --> 00:21:34,410
Mark my words.

384
00:21:34,410 --> 00:21:36,870
When only the strong remain,

385
00:21:36,870 --> 00:21:39,520
Kenjaku is going to drop
a bomb on the game,

386
00:21:39,520 --> 00:21:43,270
and the Culling Game
will fulfill its purpose.

387
00:21:54,830 --> 00:21:56,580
Please have a seat.

388
00:22:05,710 --> 00:22:07,340
Thank you for inviting me here.

389
00:23:40,730 --> 00:23:42,450
Now, make your argument.

390
00:23:42,450 --> 00:23:43,530
What happens if I'm judged guilty?

391
00:23:43,530 --> 00:23:44,960
Judgeman, please begin.

392
00:23:44,960 --> 00:23:45,890
You mean I can lie?

393
00:23:45,890 --> 00:23:47,610
You get one chance to make a statement.

394
00:23:47,610 --> 00:23:48,840
I can't control cursed energy!

395
00:23:48,840 --> 00:23:51,780
How can he hold his own against
me without cursed energy?

396
00:23:51,780 --> 00:23:53,900
I want a retrial! One more time!

397
00:23:51,990 --> 00:23:55,060
Next Episode
Tokyo No. 1 Colony, Part 3
